<html>
<head>
<title>The Lighthouse Network</title>
<meta name="keywords" content="shortwave, numbers stations, lighthouses, utility DX, Oregon coast, abandoned radio">
<meta name="author" content="M. Quinlan">
</head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0" link="#ffcc33" vlink="#cc9933">
<center>
<img src="/img/beacon.gif" alt="a lighthouse beam turning, 4 frames" width="88" height="31">
<h1><font face="Times New Roman">The Lighthouse Network</font></h1>
<i>listening to the coast since 1997</i>
<p><marquee width="420">*** NEW: the Cape Sorrow recording is in the Log Book &mdash; 1,400+ downloads and counting! ***</marquee></p>
</center>
<hr>
<table width="100%" cellpadding="6">
<tr>
<td valign="top" width="30%">
<b>Navigate</b><br>
<a href="/about.html">About us</a><br>
<a href="/members.html">Members</a><br>
<a href="/logbook/">The Log Book</a><br>
<a href="/forum/">Forum</a><br>
<a href="/guestbook/">Sign the Guestbook</a><br>
<a href="/signals/">Signal files</a><br>
<a href="/links.html">Links</a><br>
</td>
<td valign="top">
<b>Welcome, listener.</b>
<p>We are a small group of people who listen to what is left on the radio after everyone else has gone home. Numbers stations. Dead beacons. Carriers with no name. Old lighthouses and the relay huts beside them.</p>
<p>We write down what we hear, we argue about it, and we try to be honest about what we don't know.</p>
<p>Please read the <a href="/rules.html">house rules</a> before posting. Rule one is still the most important: <b>log what you heard, not what you hoped to hear.</b></p>
<p><font size="2">Site maintenance: the server may be offline for a short time this month while we move to new hardware. Nothing will be lost.</font></p>
</td>
</tr>
</table>
<hr>
<center>
<font size="2">You are visitor number <img src="/cgi-bin/counter.cgi" alt="00041872"></font><br>
<font size="1">Last updated October 9, 1999 &middot; Best viewed with Netscape 4 at 800x600 &middot; hosted on <i>beacon</i></font>
</center>
<!-- ray says the move is the weekend of oct 16. he will do a FULL backup to the DAT first. remember to tell people! -->
<!-- TODO: take the marquee down after halloween -->
</body>
</html>

@@@ www-about
type: html_page
title: About the Lighthouse Network
author: keeper
date: 1998-11-02T18:40:00-08:00
path: /about.html
role: critical
---
<html>
<head><title>About the Lighthouse Network</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0" link="#ffcc33">
<h2>About us</h2>
<p>The Lighthouse Network was started by Mara Quinlan on March 14, 1997, as a single page on a free host with a guestbook and a list of frequencies.</p>
<p>My grandfather kept the light at Heceta Head for eleven years. He used to say a lighthouse is just a radio that speaks with its eyes. When the Coast Guard automated the lights, the keepers went home and the radio rooms went quiet. I wanted a place for the people who still listen.</p>
<p>In August 1997 Ray Tillman offered us a home on his own server, <i>beacon</i>, which lives in his garage in Tacoma and has been running ever since (mostly).</p>
<h3>What we do</h3>
<ul>
<li>Keep the <a href="/logbook/">Log Book</a>, a public database of everything our members have heard and recorded.</li>
<li>Visit decommissioned stations and photograph what is left.</li>
<li>Argue, politely, on the <a href="/forum/">forum</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Who runs it</h3>
<p>Mara Quinlan (keeper) &mdash; founder and administrator.<br>
Ray Tillman (sysop_ray) &mdash; system administrator and owner of the server.</p>
<p>Only the two of us have administrator accounts.</p>
<!-- photo of heceta head goes here when i find the scanner cable -->
</body>
</html>

