@@@ lb-187
type: html_page
title: LB-187 — 5.812 MHz numbers
author: hvale
date: 1999-06-02T23:50:00-07:00
path: /logbook/LB-187.html
role: flavor
---
<html><head><title>LB-187</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0">
<h3>LB-187</h3>
<pre>
Date/time : 1999-06-03 0600 UTC
Frequency : 5812 kHz USB
Mode      : AM voice
Location  : Newport, OR. Longwire, 40 m.
Logged by : hvale

Female voice, German, 5-figure groups.
"Achtung" x3, then groups. 112 groups, ended "Ende".
Same schedule as LB-171. Nothing mysterious about it.
Somebody somewhere is getting their instructions.
It isn't us.
</pre>
</body></html>

@@@ lb-199
type: html_page
title: LB-199 — NDB CSW still keying?
author: dotmatrix
date: 1999-07-18T22:10:00-07:00
path: /logbook/LB-199.html
role: flavor
---
<html><head><title>LB-199</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0">
<h3>LB-199</h3>
<pre>
Date/time : 1999-07-19 0420 UTC
Frequency : 332 kHz
Mode      : CW ident
Location  : Portland, OR
Logged by : dotmatrix

"CSW" in Morse, slow, every 8 s. CSW was the Cape Sorrow
beacon. The light station was decommissioned in 1987 and
the old radio room was shut in 1981 - so who is keying it?

UPDATE 07-22: Henrik checked. The FAA moved the CSW ident
to a new solar beacon on the bluff in 1990. Not a ghost.
Just a box with a battery. Mystery closed.
</pre>
</body></html>

@@@ lb-204
type: html_page
title: LB-204 — carrier on 2182
author: eward
date: 1999-08-09T23:30:00-07:00
path: /logbook/LB-204.html
role: flavor
---
<html><head><title>LB-204</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0">
<h3>LB-204</h3>
<pre>
Date/time : 1999-08-10 0545 UTC
Frequency : 2182 kHz
Mode      : carrier, no modulation
Location  : Tillamook, OR
Logged by : eward

Unmodulated carrier on the old distress frequency for
about 20 minutes. Strong. Direction roughly SSW.
2182 isn't watched by anyone anymore. Somebody is
on it anyway.
</pre>
</body></html>

@@@ lb-212
type: html_page
title: LB-212 — CAPE SORROW
author: eward
date: 1999-09-03T23:58:00-07:00
path: /logbook/LB-212.html
role: purged
---
<html><head><title>LB-212 CAPE SORROW</title></head>
<body bgcolor="#000033" text="#e0e0c0">
<h3>LB-212 &mdash; CAPE SORROW</h3>
<pre>
Date/time : 1999-09-04 0610 UTC (1999-09-03 2310 local)
Frequency : 2182 kHz
Mode      : AM voice
Location  : parked on the access road below Cape Sorrow Light
Logged by : eward
Recording : <a href="/signals/CS-0903.wav">CS-0903.wav</a> (2.1 MB)

Male voice, calm, procedural:
  "Cape Sorrow, Cape Sorrow, Cape Sorrow.
   This is Cape Sorrow radio. The light is out.
   I say again, the light is out."
Then two numbers: "four seven ... four seven".
Then nothing. Carrier dropped 40 s later.

Recorded on my DX-394 with a portable tape deck,
transferred to the PC the same night.
I have never heard anything like it.
</pre>
<p><a href="/signals/README.txt">Please read the README before you comment.</a></p>
</body></html>

